Introduction
Travel planning involves selecting destinations, deciding travel dates, preparing itineraries, estimating expenses, and organizing reservations. When these activities are performed manually, users may need to visit several websites or contact travel agencies to collect information. This process can be time-consuming and difficult to manage. The Travel Planning and Booking System is proposed as a centralized web application that allows users to explore destinations, compare estimated travel costs, create trip plans, and submit booking requests. The application aims to make travel planning more organized, accessible, and convenient. 
1.1	Problem Statement 
Traditional travel planning can involve scattered information, repetitive searches, manual itinerary preparation, and difficulty tracking bookings. Users may also struggle to organize destination details and travel dates in one place. Therefore, a web-based system is proposed to integrate destination discovery, trip planning, and booking management into a single application. 



Objectives
The main objectives are: 
•	To provide destination listings with images and descriptions. 
•	To implement destination search and filtering options. 
•	To allow users to select destinations and travel dates. 
•	To support trip creation and itinerary management. 
•	To provide a booking request and confirmation workflow. 
•	To store user, trip, destination, and booking information in a database. 
•	To develop backend APIs for dynamic data handling. 
•	To validate user inputs and protect private booking information. 
•	To test the application for functionality, usability, and reliability.
Scope of the Project
The proposed system includes a homepage, destination listing page, search and filtering functions, user authentication, trip planning, booking management, and database connectivity. The application can be developed as a responsive website accessible through desktop and mobile browsers. Online payments, real-time supplier reservations, and automatic cancellation or refund processing are considered future enhancements unless separately implemented.
Significance
The project demonstrates how web technologies can combine several travel-related activities into a single interface. It also provides practical experience in application architecture, database design, API development, user interface implementation, and software testing.  
Existing System
In traditional travel planning, users may collect destination information from travel websites, social media, brochures, and travel agents. Itinerary details and booking references may be stored separately. Although online travel platforms provide useful services, a student-developed application can demonstrate how destination information, personal trip plans, and booking records can be organized within a unified system. 
