Database Design and Module Description
The database provides persistent storage for user profiles, destinations, trip plans, and booking records. A relational database such as MySQL can maintain relationships through primary and foreign keys.
Table	Important Fields	Purpose
Users	user_id, full_name, email, password_hash	Stores user account information.
Destinations	destination_id, name, country, description, estimated_cost	Stores destination details.
Trips	trip_id, user_id, trip_name, start_date, end_date	Stores user trip plans.
Trip_Destinations	trip_destination_id, trip_id, destination_id, visit_order	Connects trips with destinations and maintains visit order.
Bookings	booking_id, user_id, trip_id, booking_reference, total_amount, booking_status	Stores booking information.

Relationships
•	One user can create multiple trips. 
•	One trip can contain multiple destinations. 
•	One destination can be included in multiple trips. 
•	The Trip_Destinations table represents the many-to-many relationship between trips and destinations. 
•	One user can have multiple booking records. 
•	Each booking refers to a valid user and trip.
Database Integrity and Security
Primary keys uniquely identify records; while foreign keys help maintain valid relationships. A unique constraint should be applied to email addresses and booking references. Travel dates should be validated to ensure that the end date is not earlier than the start date. Booking totals should be calculated or verified on the backend. Passwords must be stored as secure hashes rather than plain text. Database queries should use parameterized statements to reduce SQL injection risks.
System Modules
Destination Management 
Displays destination names, countries, images, descriptions, and estimated costs. Users can browse available destinations and open their details. 
Search and Filtering 
Allows users to search by destination name and filter by supported criteria such as country or estimated budget.       
User Authentication  
Supports account registration and login. The backend validates credentials and controls access to private trip and booking information.
Trip Planning  
Allows users to select destinations, choose travel dates, and organize their planned itinerary.  

Booking Management  
Accepts booking requests, validates trip information, stores booking records, and provides a booking reference or status when the operation succeeds.  
Backend and Database Integration 
The backend processes API requests and communicates with the database. Database records should remain available after the application is restarted.
Backend API and Software Testing
REST APIs allow the frontend and backend to exchange structured information through HTTP requests and JSON responses. The following endpoints are proposed examples. Actual endpoint names depend on the implementation.
Method	Endpoint	Purpose
POST	/api/auth/register	Register a new user.
POST	/api/auth/login	Authenticate a user.
GET	/api/destinations	Retrieve all destinations.
GET	/api/destinations/:id	Retrieve details of a specific destination.
POST	/api/trips	Create a new trip.
GET	/api/trips	Retrieve trips of the authorized user.
PUT	/api/trips/:id	Update an existing trip.
POST	/api/bookings	Submit a booking request.
GET	/api/bookings	Retrieve bookings of the authorized user.
GET	/api/bookings/:id	Retrieve details of a specific booking.


